<h2>Legal preparation and coordination</h2>

We are coordinating legal preparation in the UK, with local leads joining as other regions come on line. Tim is helping lead this work alongside new volunteers. It is preparation, not a claim: any representation, proceedings or costs would need separate, explicit authority from eligible members.

If you may want to take part in a future UK process, please make sure your <a href="/member/account/">legal name and formal contact address are recorded privately in My Data</a>. That helps avoid delay if members later choose to take a defined next step.

<h2>More member stories, wider reach</h2>

We have built a member-stories feature so owners can prepare an account of their experience with photos or video. With separate publication permission, a story can help our website, social channels and press packs. It is in final testing: <a href="/member/account/">register interest in trying new member features</a> if you would like to help test it before wider release.

Thank you to Olé, who is helping coordinate Northern Europe, and Kevin, who is helping coordinate Southern Europe — and to everyone else giving time, evidence or introductions. The more accurately we can describe what owners experience, the harder it is to treat every case as isolated.

<h2>Evidence: document imports next</h2>

We are working on a secure in-browser way to import service information and documents into your private record. It is still in development, but the aim is simple: make it easier to retain the facts that matter without emailing sensitive documents around.

<h2>The unanswered battery question</h2>

JLR says it has handled hundreds of Executive Office contacts, and members tell us many have now received a broadly standard response. It says each case will be considered individually, including relevant warranty or goodwill factors. That does not yet answer the practical question many owners are asking: if a module triggers the familiar reduced-charge symptoms and diagnostic codes outside the high-voltage warranty, is it treated as a safety concern or as chargeable degradation?

The cost of diagnosis and additional testing can run from hundreds towards the low thousands of pounds before any replacement is agreed. If several modules are involved, owners understandably question the cost against the value of the car.

Members have reported that H572 may be announced shortly, but JLR has not published its scope, timing or remedy. Some members report H571 being more sensitive than earlier software, including restrictions despite a 90% charge cap. Those are member reports, not fleet-wide data. The central question remains: if modules can still fail after software is applied, what support will be available when a car is outside its eight-year battery warranty?

